commons bynk.validation {
	---
	The rule a refinement check enforces, with its argument, as `e.rule` on a
	`ValidationError`: `MaxLength(limit: 280)` for `String where MaxLength(280)`.
	It is closed, so a `match` on it is checked for exhaustiveness and a new
	predicate is a visible change; the `_` arm is the way to opt out.

	`WellFormed` is a value that is not a valid member of its base type at all (a
	number that is not finite, an `Int` outside the safe range), before any
	predicate was tried. `message` still says which.

	Its variants name a `ValidationRule` only where the context says so (a `match`
	or `is` on one, an expected type, the other side of `==` or `!=`): a bare
	`Positive` is never a `ValidationRule` on its own, so a type of your own with a
	`Positive` or `MaxLength` variant is unaffected. `ValidationRule.MaxLength(…)`
	always works.
	---
	type ValidationRule =
		| NonNegative
		| Positive
		| InRange(lo: Bound, hi: Bound)
		| AtLeast(bound: Bound)
		| AtMost(bound: Bound)
		| NonEmpty
		| MinLength(limit: Int)
		| MaxLength(limit: Int)
		| Length(limit: Int)
		| Matches(pattern: String)
		| IsoDate
		| Cron
		| WellFormed

	---
	A numeric argument of a rule, of whichever base the refined type has: `Int`,
	`Float`, `Decimal` or `Duration`.
	---
	type Bound =
		| IntBound(value: Int)
		| FloatBound(value: Float)
		| DecimalBound(value: Decimal)
		| DurationBound(value: Duration)
}
